n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha (2-8x+4{x}^{2}+8x-8{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=5.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1043 #edb/ref 17592186447086 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1043 #edb/ref 17592186447087 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1043 #edb/ref 17592186447088 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1043 #edb/ref 17592186447089 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1043 #edb/ref 17592186447090 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1045 "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Exponential Forcing" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1046 #edb/ref 17592186447090 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050805 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447059 1053 #uuid "dbc6f2c2-31aa-5586-9a34-11c5fa68bf80" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447060 1080 "y={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447060 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447060 1083 #uuid "1b6a1591-a009-5bfe-b387-44fc5cfadb97" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447061 1080 "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}-10x{e}^{-x}" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447061 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447061 1083 #uuid "1e7337fc-999b-5131-8631-92e01aaf02d8" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447062 1080 "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447062 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447062 1