 1077 "selection" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447081 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447062 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447081 1079 #uuid "a1a20dc0-72d2-5e4c-9083-07d17fe06487" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447064 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447065 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447066 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447074 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447076 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1077 "selection" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447074 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447082 1079 #uuid "b2016737-56b8-5480-9925-df39e2b6f1f4" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447081 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1069 "q-93690" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1072 #uuid "15f1d281-e0ee-5d82-9eed-6ca553720ad8" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447083 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-{x}^{2}){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}+2\\alpha (2x-{x}^{2}){e}^{-x}+\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha (2-4x+{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}){e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447082 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1069 "q-8035" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1072 #uuid "42225f24-8460-51a6-92c2-adb948ec3f96" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447084 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha (x-1){e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha (x-1){e}^{-2x}+5(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4x-4+5-10x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447080 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1069 "q-64224" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1072 #uuid "e0127162-0b7c-5790-9745-aeb2f8fdc336" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447085 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-2x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =5$ and $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2{e}^{5x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $5$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (5x+1){e}^{5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25x+10){e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25x+10){e}^{5x}-3(\\alpha (5x+1){e}^{5x})-10(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ (25x+10-15x-3-10x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha  & =2 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{7}x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447086 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447088 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447086 1062 #uuid "20c8b2d2-f5dc-5010-aeca-b21664e05b1d" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447086 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088390 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447087 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447089 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447087 1062 #uuid "4e7b9fd0-5a09-5a0f-8a80-f1f2ee4aff2e" 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447087 1063 #edb/ref 17592186447085 13194139534991 17592186045417 true] [17592186447088 1